WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.170 --> 00:00:07.450
DAVID WINER: Hi, my
name's David Winer.

00:00:07.450 --> 00:00:11.020
I'm a product manager at Google
focused on the Kotlin language,

00:00:11.020 --> 00:00:14.150
compiler, and libraries.

00:00:14.150 --> 00:00:16.149
JAMES WARD: Hi, I'm
James Ward, a developer

00:00:16.149 --> 00:00:18.730
advocate at Google Cloud, and
I've been doing a lot of Kotlin

00:00:18.730 --> 00:00:21.110
lately.

00:00:21.110 --> 00:00:24.110
DAVID WINER: And we're here to
talk today about what Kotlin is

00:00:24.110 --> 00:00:27.350
and why it's a great
language for Cloud.

00:00:27.350 --> 00:00:29.513
So let's get started.

00:00:29.513 --> 00:00:30.930
First, we want to
give an overview

00:00:30.930 --> 00:00:36.110
of what Kotlin looks like and
what some of its features are.

00:00:36.110 --> 00:00:39.890
Kotlin is a JVM language,
so it looks a lot like Java

00:00:39.890 --> 00:00:42.740
in some ways and uses a
lot of the same machinery

00:00:42.740 --> 00:00:44.090
under the hood.

00:00:44.090 --> 00:00:46.890
There are some cool kind of
differentiating features,

00:00:46.890 --> 00:00:47.930
though.

00:00:47.930 --> 00:00:51.230
The first is that nullability
is baked into the type

00:00:51.230 --> 00:00:54.230
system in Kotlin, so this
question mark operator

00:00:54.230 --> 00:00:56.330
that you see here
actually makes it

00:00:56.330 --> 00:00:59.150
so the compiler
and tooling catches

00:00:59.150 --> 00:01:01.730
a lot of the potential null
pointer exceptions you might

00:01:01.730 --> 00:01:04.510
be used to seeing at runtime.

00:01:04.510 --> 00:01:07.430
Kotlin also has first-class
support for lambdas

00:01:07.430 --> 00:01:10.370
through this nice
trailing lambda syntax.

00:01:10.370 --> 00:01:13.010
And functional programming
is a really core part

00:01:13.010 --> 00:01:16.740
of Kotlin as a language.

00:01:16.740 --> 00:01:20.127
Kotlin also has this
template expression syntax

00:01:20.127 --> 00:01:22.210
that make it so you don't
have to mess with string

00:01:22.210 --> 00:01:24.570
concatenation as much.

00:01:24.570 --> 00:01:27.280
And this is a small one,
but semicolons are optional,

00:01:27.280 --> 00:01:32.260
so one less thing to think
about when you're programming.

00:01:32.260 --> 00:01:34.540
There are a lot
more larger features

00:01:34.540 --> 00:01:37.960
that are kind of core to
what Kotlin is as a language.

00:01:37.960 --> 00:01:40.960
Kotlin has coroutines, which
are these great primitives

00:01:40.960 --> 00:01:43.060
for writing, reading,
and reasoning

00:01:43.060 --> 00:01:45.130
about concurrent code.

00:01:45.130 --> 00:01:47.980
It's also 100%
interoperable to Java,

00:01:47.980 --> 00:01:50.590
so you don't have to migrate
your whole project over

00:01:50.590 --> 00:01:52.330
in order to start using Kotlin.

00:01:52.330 --> 00:01:56.200
You can start with just
one class or one file.

00:01:56.200 --> 00:01:58.870
Kotlin is also
expressive and concise.

00:01:58.870 --> 00:02:01.210
We routinely hear
from teams how nice

00:02:01.210 --> 00:02:04.120
it is to start writing in
Kotlin because you can say more

00:02:04.120 --> 00:02:06.130
with less code.

00:02:06.130 --> 00:02:10.210
And last but not least,
Kotlin's trailing lambda syntax,

00:02:10.210 --> 00:02:12.730
which you kind of just
saw an example of,

00:02:12.730 --> 00:02:14.950
lets you create
domain-specific languages,

00:02:14.950 --> 00:02:17.150
or DSLs, really easily.

00:02:17.150 --> 00:02:19.360
This lets you do
things in a concise way

00:02:19.360 --> 00:02:23.200
that you wouldn't expect from
a type-safe JVM language,

00:02:23.200 --> 00:02:26.200
like generating markup,
configuring your build,

00:02:26.200 --> 00:02:31.270
or really concisely configuring
routes for a web server.

00:02:31.270 --> 00:02:34.840
So we talked about what
Kotlin is and now wanted

00:02:34.840 --> 00:02:36.970
to dive into the
history of Kotlin

00:02:36.970 --> 00:02:41.410
and why teams are adopting it.

00:02:41.410 --> 00:02:42.910
The Kotlin project
was originally

00:02:42.910 --> 00:02:45.490
created 10 years
ago, but it really

00:02:45.490 --> 00:02:48.280
started accelerating in 2016.

00:02:48.280 --> 00:02:50.860
That's when 1.0 was
released and Android

00:02:50.860 --> 00:02:54.580
announced official
support for it in 2017.

00:02:54.580 --> 00:02:56.740
It grew really
quickly on Android,

00:02:56.740 --> 00:02:58.960
which is one of the
largest Java language

00:02:58.960 --> 00:03:00.530
platforms in the world.

00:03:00.530 --> 00:03:04.570
And by 2019, more than
50% of the top 1,000 apps

00:03:04.570 --> 00:03:07.420
on the Play Store
were using Kotlin.

00:03:07.420 --> 00:03:09.190
And this year
Kotlin pulled ahead

00:03:09.190 --> 00:03:12.070
to become the number
two language on the JVM,

00:03:12.070 --> 00:03:13.390
ahead of Scala.

00:03:13.390 --> 00:03:16.270
It's routinely reported as
the number one fastest growing

00:03:16.270 --> 00:03:18.460
language in the
world, and we'll look

00:03:18.460 --> 00:03:21.230
at a couple of concrete
examples of that.

00:03:21.230 --> 00:03:24.190
So here's just a sampling of
companies that are using Kotlin

00:03:24.190 --> 00:03:26.920
in production today, and
you'll notice that many of them

00:03:26.920 --> 00:03:28.550
are mobile-first companies.

00:03:28.550 --> 00:03:31.360
A lot of them started
using Kotlin on Android,

00:03:31.360 --> 00:03:34.490
but many are growing it on
the server side as well.

00:03:34.490 --> 00:03:36.430
The main takeaway
here is that Kotlin

00:03:36.430 --> 00:03:38.920
has had a lot of time
to incubate and grow

00:03:38.920 --> 00:03:41.290
through usage in some of
these flagship companies.

00:03:41.290 --> 00:03:42.960
It's production ready.

00:03:42.960 --> 00:03:45.790
And at Google, we're also
going all in on Kotlin.

00:03:45.790 --> 00:03:49.090
It's our preferred language for
Android app development now.

00:03:49.090 --> 00:03:51.490
And here's an example of
just some of the results

00:03:51.490 --> 00:03:53.620
the Google Home
app team saw when

00:03:53.620 --> 00:03:57.670
moving 100% of their new
feature development to Kotlin.

00:03:57.670 --> 00:04:01.780
They saw a 33% reduction in
the null pointer exceptions

00:04:01.780 --> 00:04:03.830
they saw from their app.

00:04:03.830 --> 00:04:08.080
And when they moved classes
from Java to Kotlin,

00:04:08.080 --> 00:04:12.730
they saw on average a 30%
reduction in the lines of code.

00:04:12.730 --> 00:04:14.590
Additionally, we're
also seeing a lot

00:04:14.590 --> 00:04:16.950
of teams internally
within Google adopt

00:04:16.950 --> 00:04:19.910
Kotlin on the server side now.

00:04:19.910 --> 00:04:23.200
So metrics teams, a number
of our logging teams,

00:04:23.200 --> 00:04:25.390
platform teams for a number
of our largest products

00:04:25.390 --> 00:04:27.400
have just started
introducing Kotlin.

00:04:27.400 --> 00:04:28.960
And the internal
server framework

00:04:28.960 --> 00:04:33.170
that powers Google applications
is migrating to Kotlin as well.

00:04:33.170 --> 00:04:36.680
Lastly, I wanted to bring
the concision point to life.

00:04:36.680 --> 00:04:38.860
This is one of the
reasons that people

00:04:38.860 --> 00:04:41.965
seem to enjoy programming
in Kotlin the most.

00:04:41.965 --> 00:04:44.260
So what you see here
is a plain old Java

00:04:44.260 --> 00:04:48.070
object that you might notice
has a lot of boilerplate code--

00:04:48.070 --> 00:04:49.270
PersonJava.

00:04:49.270 --> 00:04:53.320
So it has getters, setters,
an equals method, hash code,

00:04:53.320 --> 00:04:54.400
and a lot more.

00:04:54.400 --> 00:04:56.530
It might look familiar to you.

00:04:56.530 --> 00:04:59.840
Now, here's the exact
same object in Kotlin,

00:04:59.840 --> 00:05:03.670
and you might be wondering what
happened to all the extra code.

00:05:03.670 --> 00:05:05.710
Kotlin language
features take care

00:05:05.710 --> 00:05:08.660
of generating the
necessary bytecode for you,

00:05:08.660 --> 00:05:12.100
so those getters, setters,
hash code are still there.

00:05:12.100 --> 00:05:15.230
Only you don't have to worry
about handwriting them.

00:05:15.230 --> 00:05:18.100
Instead, you can spend time
being creative, implementing

00:05:18.100 --> 00:05:20.690
new features that your
customers actually need,

00:05:20.690 --> 00:05:22.690
and not worrying about
writing hundreds of lines

00:05:22.690 --> 00:05:24.790
of boilerplate.

00:05:24.790 --> 00:05:26.800
So now I'll hand
things over to James

00:05:26.800 --> 00:05:30.430
to talk about how Kotlin can
work with some of the server

00:05:30.430 --> 00:05:36.040
tools, frameworks, and APIs
that you might already be using.

00:05:36.040 --> 00:05:38.052
JAMES WARD: So Kotlin
works great on a server

00:05:38.052 --> 00:05:38.760
and on the cloud.

00:05:38.760 --> 00:05:40.920
It works great on Google
Cloud, so if you're

00:05:40.920 --> 00:05:43.440
using just raw compute
instances or you're

00:05:43.440 --> 00:05:47.280
using Kubernetes or Cloud Run
or any of the other compute

00:05:47.280 --> 00:05:51.000
platforms, Kotlin is awesome
because it runs on the JVM,

00:05:51.000 --> 00:05:53.730
it interoperates
with Java, and a lot

00:05:53.730 --> 00:05:55.890
of the modern
Java-based frameworks

00:05:55.890 --> 00:05:58.300
are now supporting
Kotlin as well.

00:05:58.300 --> 00:06:01.850
So, for example, Spring Boot
has added great Kotlin support.

00:06:01.850 --> 00:06:04.560
There's a new framework
called Micronaut that

00:06:04.560 --> 00:06:07.120
also has great Kotlin support.

00:06:07.120 --> 00:06:10.177
There's support now
in Kotlin for gRPC,

00:06:10.177 --> 00:06:12.510
and then there's a [? really ?]
[? new ?] project called

00:06:12.510 --> 00:06:13.350
Cloudstate.

00:06:13.350 --> 00:06:15.320
And so we'll go
over some of these,

00:06:15.320 --> 00:06:17.940
but let's start
with Spring Boot.

00:06:17.940 --> 00:06:21.540
Spring Boot is the most
popular enterprise framework,

00:06:21.540 --> 00:06:24.570
and usually people are
using that with Java.

00:06:24.570 --> 00:06:26.860
But now with the
new Kotlin support,

00:06:26.860 --> 00:06:28.560
you can use Kotlin
with Spring Boot,

00:06:28.560 --> 00:06:29.890
and it feels really natural.

00:06:29.890 --> 00:06:32.395
You still get the
annotation-based injection

00:06:32.395 --> 00:06:34.270
and all the other great
parts of Spring Boot.

00:06:34.270 --> 00:06:37.050
But then you, as well as that,
get all the features of Kotlin,

00:06:37.050 --> 00:06:39.120
including coroutines,
null safety,

00:06:39.120 --> 00:06:41.280
and all that great stuff.

00:06:41.280 --> 00:06:43.290
So let's actually
take a look at a demo.

00:06:43.290 --> 00:06:46.190
Let's look at some code and
deploy a Spring Boot Kotlin

00:06:46.190 --> 00:06:47.690
application on the cloud.

00:06:47.690 --> 00:06:51.260
Let's take a look at a little
demo of a Spring Boot Kotlin

00:06:51.260 --> 00:06:53.810
application and run
it in the cloud.

00:06:53.810 --> 00:06:55.760
I'm on a repo here on GitHub--

00:06:55.760 --> 00:06:59.157
jamesward and then
springboot-kotlin-reactive-demo.

00:06:59.157 --> 00:07:00.740
And before we take
a look at the code,

00:07:00.740 --> 00:07:03.170
let's go get this thing
deploying up on the cloud.

00:07:03.170 --> 00:07:05.720
One way to do that is to
hit this Run on Google Cloud

00:07:05.720 --> 00:07:07.400
button, so I hit that.

00:07:07.400 --> 00:07:09.080
And now it's going
to say, are you sure?

00:07:09.080 --> 00:07:12.980
I'm going to confirm, and it's
now launching Cloud Shell.

00:07:12.980 --> 00:07:18.200
Cloud Shell is a VM in the
cloud with a SSH-like web

00:07:18.200 --> 00:07:19.480
interface to it.

00:07:19.480 --> 00:07:21.480
And this just allows us
to automate a few steps,

00:07:21.480 --> 00:07:23.300
so we don't have
to do much typing.

00:07:23.300 --> 00:07:25.478
So lets clone that Git repo
we were just looking at.

00:07:25.478 --> 00:07:27.770
And then it's asking me which
project do I want to use.

00:07:27.770 --> 00:07:29.930
I will use jw-demo.

00:07:29.930 --> 00:07:32.190
It's going to enable
