00:07:44.740 --> 00:07:46.490
So we'll check back
in on this in a second

00:07:46.490 --> 00:07:49.460
because it's going to take
a minute or so to do that.

00:07:49.460 --> 00:07:52.162
While it's doing that,
we can take a look

00:07:52.162 --> 00:07:53.870
at a little bit of
stuff in this project.

00:07:53.870 --> 00:07:55.280
First, there is the pom.xml.

00:07:55.280 --> 00:07:56.370
This should be familiar.

00:07:56.370 --> 00:07:57.890
This is for the Maven build.

00:07:57.890 --> 00:08:01.330
Nothing too exciting in here
other than the Kotlin stuff,

00:08:01.330 --> 00:08:03.060
Spring Boot stuff.

00:08:03.060 --> 00:08:04.700
And you'll see that
I'm using WebFlux

00:08:04.700 --> 00:08:10.220
so that we get a non-blocking
reactive HTTP server

00:08:10.220 --> 00:08:12.432
for this Spring Boot project.

00:08:12.432 --> 00:08:14.390
So nothing Kotlin yet,
but let's go take a look

00:08:14.390 --> 00:08:15.690
at the Kotlin source code.

00:08:15.690 --> 00:08:19.670
This is in the demo application
and really basic Spring Boot

00:08:19.670 --> 00:08:21.120
application here.

00:08:21.120 --> 00:08:25.280
So I've got my demo
application class, which

00:08:25.280 --> 00:08:28.250
has my router in it,
and we'll take a look

00:08:28.250 --> 00:08:30.980
at that method in a second.

00:08:30.980 --> 00:08:32.480
I'm using some
Kotlin features here,

00:08:32.480 --> 00:08:34.429
like a data class
called Release,

00:08:34.429 --> 00:08:37.010
and then I have a
function main that

00:08:37.010 --> 00:08:39.929
starts up my demo application.

00:08:39.929 --> 00:08:42.020
So what this application
does-- very simple.

00:08:42.020 --> 00:08:45.830
It's going to make an HTTP
request out to the GitHub API

00:08:45.830 --> 00:08:49.880
to fetch the tags
for Kotlin releases.

00:08:49.880 --> 00:08:52.590
And this is using
the web client,

00:08:52.590 --> 00:08:57.450
which is also a non-blocking
WebFlux-based HTTP client.

00:08:57.450 --> 00:08:59.720
So because of
Kotlin coroutines--

00:08:59.720 --> 00:09:01.580
and a great feature of Kotlin--

00:09:01.580 --> 00:09:03.570
we have a suspend function.

00:09:03.570 --> 00:09:07.220
So even though this is a
non-blocking async call,

00:09:07.220 --> 00:09:10.822
we're able to make it look
like it's just sequential.

00:09:10.822 --> 00:09:12.530
So we're going to get
our releases, which

00:09:12.530 --> 00:09:15.140
are going deserialize
into our list of release.

00:09:15.140 --> 00:09:18.380
And what I was trying to do with
this application is just get

00:09:18.380 --> 00:09:21.560
the actual releases, not
any milestone releases.

00:09:21.560 --> 00:09:24.180
So we see some other
features of Kotlin here.

00:09:24.180 --> 00:09:28.070
One is that the question
mark or the Elvis operator

00:09:28.070 --> 00:09:31.150
used for dealing with
nullability because maybe

00:09:31.150 --> 00:09:32.490
my releases were null.

00:09:32.490 --> 00:09:35.810
I didn't have any
releases, or I wasn't

00:09:35.810 --> 00:09:37.047
able to fetch the releases.

00:09:37.047 --> 00:09:39.380
Then I can do that question
mark [? so it would ?] chain

00:09:39.380 --> 00:09:40.960
on operations when
it's not null,

00:09:40.960 --> 00:09:44.690
so then I can do a filter
with a nice little function

00:09:44.690 --> 00:09:48.920
syntax there to filter out
anything that doesn't contain

00:09:48.920 --> 00:09:50.000
a dash or that--

00:09:50.000 --> 00:09:52.110
filter out all the things
that contain a dash.

00:09:52.110 --> 00:09:55.730
And then I'm going to, again,
use the nice nullability

00:09:55.730 --> 00:09:58.690
support in Kotlin,
get the firstOrNull,

00:09:58.690 --> 00:10:00.190
and if it's not
null, then I'm going

00:10:00.190 --> 00:10:02.040
to get the name of that release.

00:10:02.040 --> 00:10:05.780
And then if any of those
operations in that chain

00:10:05.780 --> 00:10:09.270
were null, then I'm going to
provide a default of not found.

00:10:09.270 --> 00:10:13.910
So some really nice features
of Kotlin here in a Spring Boot

00:10:13.910 --> 00:10:15.140
application.

00:10:15.140 --> 00:10:17.960
Let's check back in on Cloud
Shell and see how we're doing.

