Kotlin application running up

00:11:55.750 --> 00:11:57.140
on Google Cloud.

00:11:57.140 --> 00:11:57.640
OK.

00:11:57.640 --> 00:12:01.030
So that was a example of
using Spring Boot and Kotlin

00:12:01.030 --> 00:12:04.420
on the cloud, but now
let's talk about gRPC.

00:12:04.420 --> 00:12:07.330
gRPC allows us to
use protocol buffers

00:12:07.330 --> 00:12:09.980
to define our data objects.

00:12:09.980 --> 00:12:13.000
So we have a language agnostic
way to define something

00:12:13.000 --> 00:12:15.480
like a greeter service
and then the messages

00:12:15.480 --> 00:12:18.230
that that service can handle.

00:12:18.230 --> 00:12:21.220
So this allows us to use
gRPC with any language.

00:12:21.220 --> 00:12:24.720
It's protocol agnostic,
support strong typing.

00:12:24.720 --> 00:12:29.980
It'll generate the objects
around those services

00:12:29.980 --> 00:12:32.080
and messages, and
then it'll also

00:12:32.080 --> 00:12:34.570
generate the clients
and service objects

00:12:34.570 --> 00:12:38.440
to interact with those services
from a client to a server.

00:12:38.440 --> 00:12:44.100
And best of all, it's super fast
because it's built on HTTP/2.

00:12:44.100 --> 00:12:48.990
So we now support Kotlin with
gRPC through a project called

00:12:48.990 --> 00:12:51.730
gRPC Kotlin.

00:12:51.730 --> 00:12:56.720
So gRPC Kotlin, what we do is we
generate Kotlin idiomatic stubs

00:12:56.720 --> 00:12:59.000
based on the Java stubs
that are generated

00:12:59.000 --> 00:13:02.410
by the Java gRPC project.

00:13:02.410 --> 00:13:05.280
So this supports coroutines
for doing async stuff.

00:13:05.280 --> 00:13:08.340
It supports the Kotlin
flow API for doing

00:13:08.340 --> 00:13:10.140
stream-based processing.

00:13:10.140 --> 00:13:13.830
So that means that your services
can take a stream as an input

00:13:13.830 --> 00:13:16.230
and/or return a
stream as an output,

00:13:16.230 --> 00:13:20.260
and then you use the standard
flow API to interact with it.

00:13:20.260 --> 00:13:23.350
There's plugins for doing
gRPC Kotlin for both Gradle

00:13:23.350 --> 00:13:24.330
and Maven.

00:13:24.330 --> 00:13:26.590
And, of course, it's
available in Maven Central,

00:13:26.590 --> 00:13:28.600
so easy to get.

00:13:28.600 --> 00:13:32.370
So let's take a look at a demo
for gRPC Kotlin and its stream

00:13:32.370 --> 00:13:35.460
support and running on
the cloud with Cloud Run.

00:13:35.460 --> 00:13:37.380
Now let's take a look
at an example that

00:13:37.380 --> 00:13:40.860
uses gRPC and Kotlin.

00:13:40.860 --> 00:13:42.870
You can find this
on GitHub under

00:13:42.870 --> 00:13:46.980
the GoogleCloudPlatform/kotlin
samples and then run and then

00:13:46.980 --> 00:13:48.832
grpc-hello-world-streaming.

00:13:48.832 --> 00:13:50.540
There's a few different
examples in here.

00:13:50.540 --> 00:13:52.560
We're going to look
at the streaming one.

00:13:52.560 --> 00:13:56.010
And if you want to deploy
this up on Cloud Run,

00:13:56.010 --> 00:13:59.040
you can just click this
Run on Google Cloud button,

00:13:59.040 --> 00:14:01.470
and that will deploy it.

00:14:01.470 --> 00:14:04.350
And we'll let Cloud
Shell start up

00:14:04.350 --> 00:14:08.710
and then go take a look
at the source code.

00:14:08.710 --> 00:14:11.130
So in the source
code for this sample,

00:14:11.130 --> 00:14:13.530
let's first take a
look at the proto file.

00:14:13.530 --> 00:14:16.590
This is the protocol
buffer definition

00:14:16.590 --> 00:14:19.020
that defines a service
called Greeter,

00:14:19.020 --> 00:14:23.460
and rpc on that service
called SayHelloStream.

00:14:23.460 --> 00:14:28.910
It takes a HelloRequest, and it
returns a stream of HelloReply.

00:14:28.910 --> 00:14:31.830
And then you can see our
HelloRequest and HelloReply

00:14:31.830 --> 00:14:34.690
message objects down below.

00:14:34.690 --> 00:14:37.740
So the gRPC Kotlin
project will then

00:14:37.740 --> 00:14:43.110
generate the classes to interact
with the service for Kotlin,

00:14:43.110 --> 00:14:45.060
and let's go take a
look at how we actually

00:14:45.060 --> 00:14:47.880
use those generated stubs.

00:14:47.880 --> 00:14:49.980
Here we have the
HelloWorldServer,

00:14:49.980 --> 00:14:53.210
and we'll start up the server.

00:14:53.210 --> 00:14:56.010
All that the server
does is implement what

00:14:56.010 --> 00:14:59.670
happens on that sayHelloStream.

00:14:59.670 --> 00:15:02.160
So you can see it
takes a HelloRequest,

00:15:02.160 --> 00:15:05.220
and it returns a
flow of HelloReply

00:15:05.220 --> 00:15:09.450
because our gRPC returned
that stream of HelloReply.

00:15:09.450 --> 00:15:15.000
Flow in Kotlin is a
way to do a stream

00:15:15.000 --> 00:15:18.120
with non-blocking
back pressure and all

00:15:18.120 --> 00:15:19.540
that kind of good stuff.

00:15:19.540 --> 00:15:22.530
So we're going to
emit a HelloReply,

00:15:22.530 --> 00:15:25.260
and what we're going
to do in that reply

00:15:25.260 --> 00:15:28.890
is create a new HelloReply
from the builder

00:15:28.890 --> 00:15:31.800
and set the message to hello
and then whatever name came

00:15:31.800 --> 00:15:33.250
in the request.

00:15:33.250 --> 00:15:35.710
So we're just going to
omit that every second.

00:15:35.710 --> 00:15:37.890
So that's our server
implementation.

00:15:37.890 --> 00:15:40.380
Nothing very fancy there.

00:15:40.380 --> 00:15:43.830
Then let's go take a look
at the client that uses--

00:15:43.830 --> 00:15:45.730
connects to that server.

00:15:45.730 --> 00:15:48.510
So in this case, we're going
to use the generated stubs

00:15:48.510 --> 00:15:51.870
for this gRPC service,
and we're going

00:15:51.870 --> 00:15:54.960
to have a function
in here called

00:15:54.960 --> 00:15:56.940
greet, which is
going to take a name.

00:15:56.940 --> 00:16:00.150
And then it's going to do
this runBlocking, where

00:16:00.150 --> 00:16:03.960
it's going to assemble
a HelloRequest

00:16:03.960 --> 00:16:07.170
and set the name to
whatever was passed in.

00:16:07.170 --> 00:16:10.920
And then it's going to call
the stub SayHelloStream,

00:16:10.920 --> 00:16:14.650
give it that request,
and this returns a flow.

00:16:14.650 --> 00:16:17.220
So that's the Kotlin flow.

00:16:17.220 --> 00:16:19.800
And then we're going to
do a .collect on here with

00:16:19.800 --> 00:16:22.240
a function that
takes the response.

00:16:22.240 --> 00:16:24.600
And in this case, we're
just going to print out

00:16:24.600 --> 00:16:26.930
the message that was passed in.

00:16:26.930 --> 00:16:29.340
Now, down here we can
see our main method

00:16:29.340 --> 00:16:31.920
where we start up the
client, and you'll

00:16:31.920 --> 00:16:33.570
see that when we
start up the client,

00:16:33.570 --> 00:16:35.860
we're going to-- if
there is an argument,

00:16:35.860 --> 00:16:38.280
we're going to use it, an
argument to the program.

00:16:38.280 --> 00:16:42.030
If not, we're going to just pass
in world, so you'll see what

00:16:42.030 --> 00:16:43.898
actually then gets passed in.

00:16:43.898 --> 00:16:45.690
We set the name to the
name that was there.

00:16:45.690 --> 00:16:47.670
Then if we look
back at our server,

00:16:47.670 --> 00:16:49.350
it sets the message
that it's going

00:16:49.350 --> 00:16:51.900
to emit every second
to hello comma and then

00:16:51.900 --> 00:16:54.280
the name that was passed in.

00:16:54.280 --> 00:16:56.517
So let's go back over
into the Cloud Shell.

00:16:56.517 --> 00:16:58.600
And I'm actually just going
to cancel out of this.

00:16:58.600 --> 00:17:00.480
I'm not going to go
through the deployment

00:17:00.480 --> 00:17:01.522
because we don't need to.

00:17:01.522 --> 00:17:02.500
It's already deployed.

00:17:02.500 --> 00:17:05.490
And now I'm going to run
the client against my server

00:17:05.490 --> 00:17:08.335
that's up and
running on Cloud Run.

00:17:08.335 --> 00:17:10.710
So what I'm doing is I'm taking
the Docker container that

00:17:10.710 --> 00:17:13.722
was created if we had followed
through with that process

00:17:13.722 --> 00:17:15.180
and let it run to
create the Docker

00:17:15.180 --> 00:17:18.160
container from that source code,
but I've already done that.

00:17:18.160 --> 00:17:23.605
And then it's going to run the
HelloWorldClientKt application,

00:17:23.605 --> 00:17:25.230
and then it's going
to pass an argument

00:17:25.230 --> 00:17:28.530
to the application, which
is the server to connect to.

00:17:28.530 --> 00:17:30.540
And if you'll remember,
we take that argument,

00:17:30.540 --> 00:17:33.400
and we output it after
hello every second.

00:17:33.400 --> 00:17:35.550
So let's now run that
Docker container.

00:17:35.550 --> 00:17:38.820
It's going to go grab that from
the Google Container Registry

00:17:38.820 --> 00:17:42.540
and download that to Cloud
Shell to my local Docker daemon

00:17:42.540 --> 00:17:46.660
there, and so that'll take a
second to pull all that down.

00:17:46.660 --> 00:17:48.540
And once that's
pulled down, it'll

00:17:48.540 --> 00:17:52.410
start up the Docker container,
run the HelloWorldClient,

00:17:52.410 --> 00:17:56.910
and then connect it to
our running on Cloud Run.

00:17:56.910 --> 00:17:59.830
And, remember, Cloud
Run is serverless,

00:17:59.830 --> 00:18:01.830
so it scales down when
there aren't any requests

00:18:01.830 --> 00:18:05.050
and then automatically
scales back up as needed.

00:18:05.050 --> 00:18:08.070
And you'll see that it's
started up that gRPC server,

00:18:08.070 --> 00:18:11.610
and now it's outputting every
second hello with that argument

00:18:11.610 --> 00:18:13.510
that was passed in.

00:18:13.510 --> 00:18:14.170
So there we go.

00:18:14.170 --> 00:18:16.800
That's our Kotlin
gRPC application

00:18:16.800 --> 00:18:21.840
running on Cloud Run and
doing a streaming response.

00:18:21.840 --> 00:18:23.805
So that was gRPC Kotlin.

00:18:23.805 --> 00:18:25.680
Now let's take a look
at a new project called

00:18:25.680 --> 00:18:29.670
Cloudstate, which has great
Kotlin support as well.

00:18:29.670 --> 00:18:33.030
Cloudstate was created to
support distributed programming

00:18:33.030 --> 00:18:35.730
through some new architectural
patterns called event

00:18:35.730 --> 00:18:38.610
sourcing, CQRS, and CRDTs.

00:18:38.610 --> 00:18:41.350
And I'm not going to go into all
the details of what those are,

00:18:41.350 --> 00:18:44.340
but if you want to do really
high-scale distributed

00:18:44.340 --> 00:18:47.610
computing, then you may
need architectural patterns

00:18:47.610 --> 00:18:49.080
that support that.

00:18:49.080 --> 00:18:51.510
So the new Cloudstate
project is open source

00:18:51.510 --> 00:18:54.120
and has a cloud service
built on Google Cloud

00:18:54.120 --> 00:18:56.370
that we can use
with Kotlin to build

00:18:56.370 --> 00:18:59.080
these massive-scale
applications.

00:18:59.080 --> 00:19:02.920
Let's take a look at a little
demo of Cloudstate with Kotlin.

00:19:02.920 --> 00:19:05.980
Let's take a look at a
Cloudstate Kotlin example

00:19:05.980 --> 00:19:09.340
that uses the Lightbend
Cloudstate service to run

00:19:09.340 --> 00:19:10.900
this on the cloud.

00:19:10.900 --> 00:19:14.560
And you can find the
source code on GitHub--

00:19:14.560 --> 00:19:17.400
jamesward/cloudstate
sample fraud.

00:19:17.400 --> 00:19:17.900
OK.

00:19:17.900 --> 00:19:20.390
First, let's take
a look at the code.

00:19:20.390 --> 00:19:22.720
So with Cloudstate,
we use protobufs

00:19:22.720 --> 00:19:26.330
to model the messages
that we're going to send

00:19:26.330 --> 00:19:30.830
and then the rpc operations
that will be performed.

00:19:30.830 --> 00:19:34.820
So we have a transaction with
a timestamp amount, location,

00:19:34.820 --> 00:19:36.670
and description.

00:19:36.670 --> 00:19:40.030
And then we have a user
transaction that associates

00:19:40.030 --> 00:19:42.400
that with a
cloudstate.entity_key,

00:19:42.400 --> 00:19:45.790
which allows us to do the
partition of the messages.

00:19:45.790 --> 00:19:47.260
And that also gives
us the ability

00:19:47.260 --> 00:19:50.740
to scale this thing up and
down horizontally based

00:19:50.740 --> 00:19:53.050
on what's happening
to the messages

00:19:53.050 --> 00:19:54.740
as they're being partitioned.

00:19:54.740 --> 00:19:55.240
OK.

00:19:55.240 --> 00:19:58.090
And then we have a way to
add the transaction, a user

00:19:58.090 --> 00:20:01.700
transaction, and
get transactions.

00:20:01.700 --> 00:20:05.120
Let's take a look at
the ActivityEntity,

00:20:05.120 --> 00:20:09.010
and this is where the logic
for handling those messages

00:20:09.010 --> 00:20:10.400
happens.

00:20:10.400 --> 00:20:12.630
So we've got an
EventSourcedEntity,

00:20:12.630 --> 00:20:14.380
and then you can see
there's a constructor

00:20:14.380 --> 00:20:17.530
argument with a user ID,
which is our partitioning key.

00:20:17.530 --> 00:20:20.890
So that's how we're able
to partition this data.

00:20:20.890 --> 00:20:23.900
In this class, we
have our transactions,

00:20:23.900 --> 00:20:25.510
which is our MutableList.

00:20:25.510 --> 00:20:29.590
And so whenever we get
transaction messages coming in,

00:20:29.590 --> 00:20:31.840
then we're going to be able
to add them to this list

00:20:31.840 --> 00:20:36.280
so that we have our accumulated
state here in this entity.

00:20:36.280 --> 00:20:39.460
Then we have a command handler
for addTransaction, which

00:20:39.460 --> 00:20:41.750
takes that user transaction.

00:20:41.750 --> 00:20:44.080
And then we also get a
command context [? right ?]

00:20:44.080 --> 00:20:45.020
[? there. ?]

00:20:45.020 --> 00:20:47.530
And then we can have our
logic for detecting fraud

00:20:47.530 --> 00:20:49.240
on a transaction.

00:20:49.240 --> 00:20:51.370
So whenever a new
transaction comes in,

00:20:51.370 --> 00:20:55.570
we're going to use the past
data and the new transaction

00:20:55.570 --> 00:20:58.298
to calculate the velocity.

00:20:58.298 --> 00:21:00.340
And so there's, obviously,
lots of different ways

00:21:00.340 --> 00:21:02.470
that we would calculate--

00:21:02.470 --> 00:21:04.690
compute whether or
not the transaction

00:21:04.690 --> 00:21:06.640
is potentially fraudulent.

00:21:06.640 --> 00:21:09.980
For this simple example,
I'm just using velocity.

00:21:09.980 --> 00:21:13.840
And if the user's velocity
goes over a max velocity,

00:21:13.840 --> 00:21:16.390
then we're going to say
that there's possible fraud.

00:21:16.390 --> 00:21:20.150
And in this case, we're just
going to output to the console.

00:21:20.150 --> 00:21:24.430
But then once we have
detected the potential fraud,

00:21:24.430 --> 00:21:28.330
we are going to emit a message
which says, OK, we've done

00:21:28.330 --> 00:21:29.750
our handling of this message.

00:21:29.750 --> 00:21:31.840
And then that
EventHandler will trigger.

00:21:31.840 --> 00:21:36.270
This will go on to an event log,
which has persisted in Spanner,

00:21:36.270 --> 00:21:37.970
in Google Cloud Spanner.

00:21:37.970 --> 00:21:38.470
OK.

00:21:38.470 --> 00:21:42.220
So that's the bulk of
the entity processing.

00:21:42.220 --> 00:21:45.040
I've got a [? little ?] class
that just starts up the server,

00:21:45.040 --> 00:21:47.380
and then I've got a
simulator class that is going

00:21:47.380 --> 00:21:50.780
to simulate some transactions.

00:21:50.780 --> 00:21:53.380
So let's go over
and run this now.

00:21:53.380 --> 00:21:55.060
I already have
this up and running

00:21:55.060 --> 00:22:00.160
on Google Cloud via the
Lightbend Cloudstate service,

00:22:00.160 --> 00:22:02.050
but let's go start
up the simulator,

00:22:02.050 --> 00:22:04.690
and what this will do is start
adding a bunch of transactions

00:22:04.690 --> 00:22:06.520
to this transaction log.

00:22:06.520 --> 00:22:08.050
And what we'll be able to--

00:22:08.050 --> 00:22:10.450
once this starts
emitting those messages,

00:22:10.450 --> 00:22:13.270
we'll be able to see the
messages that are being emitted

00:22:13.270 --> 00:22:14.470
here in the console.

00:22:14.470 --> 00:22:17.410
And then we'll go over
to Google Cloud Logging

00:22:17.410 --> 00:22:20.470
to actually see if we
get a potential fraud.

00:22:20.470 --> 00:22:21.280
So here we go.

00:22:21.280 --> 00:22:25.840
We're now feeding a bunch of
transactions into that system.

00:22:25.840 --> 00:22:27.640
We've got multiple
users, so we're

00:22:27.640 --> 00:22:31.230
doing partitioning of
the data for that entity.

00:22:31.230 --> 00:22:36.220
And now here in Cloud, the log
viewer in Google Cloud, now

00:22:36.220 --> 00:22:39.310
we can see the server
messages from my entity

00:22:39.310 --> 00:22:44.050
that's running up on Google
Cloud and Lightbend Cloudstate.

00:22:44.050 --> 00:22:46.850
So let's go try to jump to
now and see-- there we go.

00:22:46.850 --> 00:22:47.350
OK.

00:22:47.350 --> 00:22:50.260
So it looks like we just
got a possible fraud alert,

00:22:50.260 --> 00:22:54.160
and we can see the user ID,
the merchant, the amount, when

00:22:54.160 --> 00:22:57.880
it happened, and then
the Google Map linked

00:22:57.880 --> 00:22:59.980
to where that transaction was.

00:22:59.980 --> 00:23:02.030
And if we let this
continue running,

00:23:02.030 --> 00:23:05.620
we probably will see some more
potential fraud alerts coming

00:23:05.620 --> 00:23:08.348
in as that simulator runs.

00:23:08.348 --> 00:23:10.390
So you can see we're
feeding in tons of messages.

00:23:10.390 --> 00:23:12.430
They're partitioned
based on user ID,

00:23:12.430 --> 00:23:15.760
so we get the ability to
auto-scale the service

00:23:15.760 --> 00:23:16.780
up and down.

00:23:16.780 --> 00:23:20.150
And that's great for
something like this

00:23:20.150 --> 00:23:22.870
where the amount of
transactions that are coming in

00:23:22.870 --> 00:23:26.010
is going to fluctuate
throughout the day,

00:23:26.010 --> 00:23:29.500
with probably more transactions
happening in the morning

00:23:29.500 --> 00:23:32.320
and evening, I would
guess, and less at night.

00:23:32.320 --> 00:23:33.730
And so great.

00:23:33.730 --> 00:23:36.810
We can scale our service
automatically based on that.

00:23:36.810 --> 00:23:40.930
There's a container image
that I built and deployed,

00:23:40.930 --> 00:23:43.550
which contains my entity code.

00:23:43.550 --> 00:23:44.050
OK.

00:23:44.050 --> 00:23:46.630
So that's the quick
example of Cloudstate

00:23:46.630 --> 00:23:50.400
running on the Lightbend
Cloudstate service.

00:23:50.400 --> 00:23:52.970
So that was
Cloudstate for Kotlin.

00:23:52.970 --> 00:23:55.790
And that's it that we
wanted to show you today.

00:23:55.790 --> 00:23:57.910
Thanks for watching,
and we hope that you

00:23:57.910 --> 00:24:00.340
will build Kotlin
stuff for Google Cloud,

00:24:00.340 --> 00:24:02.260
and check out some
resources for that.

00:24:02.260 --> 00:24:05.290
You can go to
cloud.google.com/kotlin.

00:24:05.290 --> 00:24:07.720
And thanks so much, David,
for joining us and showing us

00:24:07.720 --> 00:24:09.220
why we should use Kotlin.

00:24:09.220 --> 00:24:09.970
DAVID WINER: Yeah.

00:24:09.970 --> 00:24:10.960
Thanks, James.

00:24:10.960 --> 00:24:13.540
We're really excited to see
what you build with Kotlin,

00:24:13.540 --> 00:24:17.160
and hope you enjoy the
rest of Next OnAir.

